export default [
  {"d":3,"t":"analytics","q":"A company runs an on-premises Hadoop cluster with Apache Hive and HBase jobs. It wants to move these jobs to AWS with minimal changes and shut the cluster down after each nightly run to save money. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Redshift","AWS Glue","Amazon EMR","Amazon Athena"],"a":[2],"e":"Amazon EMR runs open-source big data frameworks such as Hadoop, Hive, HBase, and Spark on managed clusters that can be transient and terminated after the job. AWS Glue is a serverless ETL service built on Spark; it does not run an existing Hive/HBase ecosystem."},
  {"d":3,"t":"analytics","q":"A data team wants to pick its own EC2 instance types, install open-source tools such as Presto and HBase, and resize the cluster as data volumes grow. Which AWS analytics service provides this level of control?","o":["Amazon EMR","Amazon Athena","Amazon Kinesis","Amazon Quick"],"a":[0],"e":"Amazon EMR gives you a managed cluster of EC2 instances where you choose instance types, applications, and cluster size. Athena is serverless SQL over S3 with no cluster to configure."},
  {"d":3,"t":"analytics","q":"Which AWS service distributes the processing of very large datasets across a resizable cluster of EC2 instances by using frameworks such as Apache Hadoop MapReduce and Apache Spark?","o":["AWS Batch","Amazon Redshift","Amazon ElastiCache","Amazon EMR"],"a":[3],"e":"Amazon EMR is the managed big data platform for Hadoop and Spark processing. AWS Batch schedules and runs batch jobs (often containers) on compute it provisions, but it does not provide big data frameworks."},
  {"d":3,"t":"analytics","q":"A company wants to run Apache Spark workloads on AWS without building the Spark environment itself. Which AWS services can run Spark jobs as a managed service? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon EMR","Amazon Quick","AWS Glue","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","Amazon Neptune"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Amazon EMR runs Spark on managed clusters (or EMR Serverless), and AWS Glue runs serverless Spark-based ETL jobs. Kinesis Data Streams ingests streaming data but does not execute Spark jobs, and Neptune is a graph database."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"A retailer wants to launch a cloud contact center within days. It wants pay-as-you-go pricing based on usage, with no upfront telephony hardware or long-term contracts. Which AWS service should it use?","o":["Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)","Amazon Connect","Amazon Lex","Amazon Polly"],"a":[1],"e":"Amazon Connect is an omnichannel cloud contact center billed by usage with no infrastructure to buy. Amazon Lex builds conversational bots that can plug into a contact center, but it is not the contact center itself."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"A company's help desk is replacing a legacy call center platform. Agents need a browser-based softphone, and incoming voice calls, chats, and tasks must be routed to agents based on their skills. Which AWS service meets these needs?","o":["Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES)","Amazon Connect","Amazon WorkSpaces","AWS IoT Core"],"a":[1],"e":"Amazon Connect provides the agent workspace, softphone, and skills-based routing for voice, chat, and tasks. Amazon WorkSpaces gives agents virtual desktops, but it does not route customer contacts."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"Contact center supervisors want real-time and historical metrics on queues and agents, plus automatic call transcription and customer sentiment analysis built into their contact center. Which service provides this?","o":["Amazon CloudWatch","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Transcribe","Amazon Connect"],"a":[3],"e":"Amazon Connect includes real-time and historical contact center metrics and built-in conversational analytics (Contact Lens) for transcripts and sentiment. Comprehend and Transcribe are building-block AI services that would need custom integration and do not provide contact center metrics."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"A bank wants callers to its phone line to say what they need in their own words, such as 'I lost my card,' instead of pressing keypad options. Which combination of AWS services should it use? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon Textract","Amazon Connect","Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES)","Amazon Lex","Amazon Rekognition"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Amazon Connect handles the calls and contact flows, and Amazon Lex adds natural-language understanding so the IVR can recognize the caller's intent. Rekognition analyzes images and video, and Textract extracts text from documents."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"A marketing team needs to send newsletters to hundreds of thousands of opted-in customers and track bounces, complaints, and delivery rates. Which AWS service should it use?","o":["Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES)","Amazon Connect","Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS)","Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)"],"a":[0],"e":"Amazon SES is built for high-volume transactional and marketing email, with bounce and complaint tracking. Amazon SNS can email topic subscribers, but it sends the same plain notification to confirmed subscribers and is not a bulk email platform."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"A company wants emails sent to support@example.com to be received by AWS, saved to Amazon S3, and processed by an AWS Lambda function. Which service can receive the incoming email and trigger these actions?","o":["Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS)","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES)","Amazon API Gateway"],"a":[2],"e":"Amazon SES can receive inbound email for verified domains and use receipt rules to store messages in S3 or invoke Lambda. EventBridge routes events between services, but it cannot act as an email server for a domain."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"A developer must send personalized, branded HTML emails, such as invoices that include each customer's name and order details, directly from application code to individual customers. Which service should the developer use?","o":["Amazon Lex","AWS Step Functions","Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)","Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES)"],"a":[3],"e":"Amazon SES sends custom email (including HTML and templates) to any recipient through an API or SMTP. SNS email delivery requires recipients to subscribe to a topic and sends the same message to all subscribers, so it does not suit personalized customer email."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"A company sends email from its application on AWS and wants to authenticate that email with DKIM and SPF and monitor sender reputation metrics such as bounce rate. Which AWS service provides these capabilities?","o":["Amazon Route 53","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES)","Amazon GuardDuty"],"a":[2],"e":"Amazon SES supports DKIM and SPF email authentication and provides reputation metrics for bounces and complaints. Route 53 can host the DNS records that DKIM and SPF use, but it does not send email or track reputation."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"An online store must send order confirmation emails to customers and also send short SMS text alerts to customers' phones when an order ships. Which AWS services should it use? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES)","Amazon Kinesis","AWS Step Functions","Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS)","Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)"],"a":[0,4],"e":"Amazon SES sends the confirmation emails, and Amazon SNS can send SMS text messages to phone numbers. Amazon SQS queues messages between application components; it does not deliver anything to customers."},
  {"d":3,"t":"compute","q":"A research lab submits thousands of containerized simulation jobs each night. It needs job queues with priorities and wants AWS to provision the right amount of compute automatically, including Spot Instances. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Lambda","AWS Batch","Amazon EMR","AWS Elastic Beanstalk"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS Batch manages job queues and scheduling and provisions EC2 (including Spot) or Fargate capacity to match the submitted jobs. Lambda is event-driven and limited to 15 minutes per invocation, which does not suit long simulations."},
  {"d":3,"t":"compute","q":"Each job in a company's nightly workload runs for 3 to 5 hours, and some jobs must not start until others finish. The company does not want to install or manage a job scheduler. Which AWS service is MOST appropriate?","o":["AWS Batch","Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","AWS Lambda","Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS)"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS Batch runs long-running batch jobs with job dependencies and manages the scheduling and compute for you. EC2 Auto Scaling adds or removes instances, but it has no concept of jobs, queues, or dependencies."},
  {"d":3,"t":"compute","q":"A company is evaluating AWS Batch for its risk-calculation jobs. Which statement about AWS Batch pricing is correct?","o":["AWS Batch charges per job submitted, regardless of compute used","AWS Batch charges a fixed monthly fee per job queue","AWS Batch has no additional charge; you pay only for the AWS resources, such as EC2 instances or Fargate, that run your jobs","AWS Batch requires a 1-year commitment to use Spot Instances"],"a":[2],"e":"There is no extra charge for AWS Batch itself; you pay for the underlying compute and storage your jobs use. Spot Instances never require a commitment, which is part of why Batch is a cost-effective way to run interruptible jobs."},
  {"d":3,"t":"compute","q":"A financial firm runs many independent end-of-day risk calculations that together need thousands of vCPUs for a few hours. The firm wants to submit the work as jobs and let AWS choose and scale the instances. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Batch","Amazon EMR","AWS Outposts","Amazon Lightsail"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS Batch is designed for large numbers of independent compute jobs and dynamically provisions the instance types and quantity needed. Amazon EMR targets big data frameworks like Spark and Hadoop rather than generic batch jobs."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A product catalog stores each item as a JSON document with different attributes per product category. The application uses MongoDB drivers and needs indexes on nested fields. Which AWS database is the BEST fit?","o":["Amazon Neptune","Amazon ElastiCache","Amazon RDS for MySQL","Amazon DocumentDB"],"a":[3],"e":"Amazon DocumentDB is a managed document database with MongoDB compatibility that stores and indexes JSON-like documents. Neptune is a graph database for relationship queries, not document storage."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"Which AWS service is a fully managed document database that stores data as JSON-like documents and automatically replicates six copies of data across three Availability Zones?","o":["Amazon Redshift","Amazon DocumentDB","Amazon Aurora","Amazon Neptune"],"a":[1],"e":"Amazon DocumentDB is the managed document database, and its storage keeps six copies across three AZs. Aurora uses a similar storage design, but it is a relational MySQL/PostgreSQL-compatible database."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A company runs self-managed MongoDB clusters on Amazon EC2 and spends too much time on patching, backups, and replica management. It wants a managed service that works with its existing MongoDB application code and query language. Which service should it choose?","o":["Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL","Amazon DocumentDB","Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon Redshift"],"a":[1],"e":"Amazon DocumentDB is MongoDB-compatible, so existing drivers and queries keep working while AWS handles patching, backups, and replication. DynamoDB is a managed NoSQL database, but it uses its own API, so the application would need to be rewritten."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A company is designing a new application. It needs a user profile store that holds JSON documents accessed through MongoDB APIs, and a recommendation engine that traverses relationships between users and products. Which AWS services should it use? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon Redshift","Amazon RDS","Amazon DocumentDB","Amazon Neptune","Amazon ElastiCache"],"a":[2,3],"e":"Amazon DocumentDB fits MongoDB-compatible JSON documents, and Amazon Neptune is a graph database for relationship traversal such as recommendations. Redshift is a data warehouse for analytics, not an operational store for profiles or graph queries."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A bank wants to detect fraud rings in real time by analyzing links among accounts, devices, phone numbers, and IP addresses that are several hops apart. Which database service is MOST appropriate?","o":["Amazon Redshift","Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon Aurora","Amazon Neptune"],"a":[3],"e":"Amazon Neptune is a graph database optimized for queries that traverse many relationships, which is how fraud rings are uncovered. DynamoDB is a key-value and document store that is fast for lookups by key but not for multi-hop relationship queries."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A pharmaceutical company is building a knowledge graph that links genes, proteins, and drugs using the RDF data model and SPARQL queries. Which AWS service should it use?","o":["Amazon Neptune","Amazon OpenSearch Service","Amazon Athena","Amazon DocumentDB"],"a":[0],"e":"Amazon Neptune supports RDF with SPARQL as well as property graphs with Gremlin and openCypher, making it the knowledge graph database. OpenSearch Service indexes documents for search and does not run SPARQL graph queries."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A development team wants a managed database that supports the Apache TinkerPop Gremlin and openCypher query languages. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon DocumentDB","Amazon Neptune","Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL-Compatible Edition"],"a":[2],"e":"Gremlin and openCypher are graph query languages, and Amazon Neptune is the AWS managed graph database that supports them. Amazon DocumentDB uses the MongoDB API for documents, not graph traversals."},
  {"d":3,"t":"database","q":"A telecom company wants to model its network topology so it can instantly find every device and customer affected when a single router fails. Which AWS database is the BEST fit for this highly connected data?","o":["Amazon Timestream","Amazon ElastiCache","Amazon RDS for Oracle","Amazon Neptune"],"a":[3],"e":"Finding everything connected to a failed node is a graph traversal, which Amazon Neptune is built for. A relational database such as RDS would need many expensive self-joins to follow the same relationships."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"A team's self-managed build servers sit idle most of the day but create long build queues during peak hours. The team wants builds that scale automatically and are billed only for the build minutes used. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS CodeDeploy","AWS CodePipeline","AWS CodeBuild","AWS Elastic Beanstalk"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS CodeBuild is a fully managed build service that runs builds concurrently and charges by build minute, so there are no idle servers. CodePipeline orchestrates release stages but does not compile or test code itself."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"A developer adds a buildspec.yml file to a repository that lists the install, build, and test commands for the project. Which AWS service reads this file to run the build?","o":["AWS CloudFormation","AWS CodeBuild","AWS CodePipeline","AWS Systems Manager"],"a":[1],"e":"The buildspec file defines the commands that AWS CodeBuild runs in its managed build environment. CloudFormation reads templates that describe infrastructure, not build commands."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"In a CI/CD workflow, a company needs a managed service to run unit tests and build a Docker image that will then be pushed to Amazon ECR. Which service performs the build?","o":["AWS CodeBuild","AWS X-Ray","Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR)","AWS CodePipeline"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS CodeBuild runs the tests and builds the container image. Amazon ECR only stores and serves the finished images; it does not build them."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"A company wants a fully managed CI/CD process on AWS. Every commit must automatically start a release that compiles and tests the code and then deploys it. Which AWS services should it use? (Choose TWO.)","o":["AWS X-Ray","AWS CodePipeline","AWS Config","AWS CloudTrail","AWS CodeBuild"],"a":[1,4],"e":"AWS CodePipeline detects the commit and orchestrates the stages, and AWS CodeBuild compiles and tests the code within the pipeline. CloudTrail records API activity for auditing; it plays no part in building or releasing code."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"A company's release process takes days because each team manually starts the next step: build, test, and deploy. The company wants every code change to move through these steps automatically by using its existing build and deployment tools. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Systems Manager","AWS CodeBuild","AWS CodePipeline","AWS CloudFormation"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS CodePipeline is a continuous delivery service that automates the release workflow and connects existing build, test, and deploy tools into stages. CodeBuild performs only the build-and-test part and does not coordinate the full release."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"A development lead wants a visual view of the release process (Source, Build, Test, Deploy) that shows which code revision is in each stage and exactly where a release failed. Which AWS service provides this?","o":["AWS CodePipeline","Amazon CloudWatch dashboards","AWS CodeBuild","AWS X-Ray"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS CodePipeline shows each stage of the release workflow, the revision moving through it, and any stage that failed. X-Ray provides a visual service map of application requests, not of software releases."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"After each successful build, a company wants the application deployed automatically to a test environment and then promoted to production once automated tests pass, all without anyone starting the steps manually. Which service should coordinate this?","o":["AWS Trusted Advisor","Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR)","AWS CodeBuild","AWS CodePipeline"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS CodePipeline models multi-stage releases, so changes move from build to test to production automatically. CodeBuild can run the tests, but it does not manage promotion across environments."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"Users report that checkout is slow. The application uses Amazon API Gateway, several AWS Lambda functions, and Amazon DynamoDB. Developers need to see, for individual requests, which downstream call adds the most latency. Which service should they use?","o":["AWS Config","AWS X-Ray","AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS CloudTrail"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS X-Ray traces each request end to end and shows how long each segment took, which reveals the slow component. CloudTrail records who made which AWS API call for auditing; it does not measure application latency."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"Which AWS service builds a service map of an application's components and their connections and highlights the nodes that are returning errors or faults?","o":["Amazon Inspector","AWS X-Ray","AWS Config","Amazon VPC Flow Logs"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS X-Ray creates a service map from trace data and flags components with errors, faults, or high latency. AWS Config tracks resource configurations and relationships, not request flows."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"A microservices application returns intermittent HTTP 500 errors. Developers want to follow the failing requests across services to find which downstream dependency causes the error. Which service helps them troubleshoot this?","o":["Amazon GuardDuty","AWS CloudTrail","AWS Health Dashboard","AWS X-Ray"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS X-Ray follows requests across services and shows where errors and exceptions occur. The AWS Health Dashboard reports AWS service events and scheduled changes, not errors in your own application code."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"A developer wants to know how long a Lambda function's calls to an external HTTP API and to Amazon DynamoDB took during one specific request. Which AWS service records this timing data?","o":["AWS X-Ray","Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights","AWS CodeBuild","AWS CloudTrail"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS X-Ray records subsegments for downstream calls, such as HTTP requests and DynamoDB operations, with their durations for each trace. CloudWatch Logs Insights queries log data, and it shows timings only if the code wrote them to the logs."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A new administrator must create a single IAM user one time. The administrator has no scripting experience and prefers a visual, point-and-click interface. Which access method is MOST appropriate?","o":["AWS CLI","AWS SDK for Python (Boto3)","AWS Management Console","AWS CloudFormation"],"a":[2],"e":"The AWS Management Console is the web-based graphical interface, which suits one-time tasks by people who are not writing code. CloudFormation is better for repeatable infrastructure deployments, not a single quick change."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"Which methods provide programmatic access to AWS services by using access keys or temporary credentials? (Choose TWO.)","o":["AWS Artifact","AWS Health Dashboard","AWS Management Console","AWS CLI","AWS SDKs"],"a":[3,4],"e":"The AWS CLI and AWS SDKs call AWS APIs programmatically and authenticate with access keys or temporary credentials. The Management Console is interactive browser access that users sign in to with a password and MFA."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A Java application must read and write items in Amazon DynamoDB at runtime. The developers want a library that handles request signing, retries, and serialization. What should they use?","o":["AWS CLI","AWS CloudFormation","AWS Management Console","AWS SDK for Java"],"a":[3],"e":"An AWS SDK provides language-specific APIs inside application code and handles signing, retries, and errors. The AWS CLI is a command-line tool for humans and scripts, not a library for application code."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A team creates test environments by clicking through the AWS Management Console. It takes hours, and the environments differ slightly each time. The team must create identical environments repeatedly in three Regions. What should the team do?","o":["Take screenshots of the console settings to copy them","Write detailed console runbooks for each engineer to follow","Define the environment as code in an AWS CloudFormation template and deploy it as a stack","Use the AWS Management Console mobile application"],"a":[2],"e":"Infrastructure as code with AWS CloudFormation makes deployments repeatable, consistent, and version-controlled across Regions. Manual console steps, even with runbooks, remain slow and error-prone for repeatable processes."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"An operations engineer wants to run a quick command from a laptop terminal to list all Amazon S3 buckets, then schedule a shell script that creates nightly Amazon EBS snapshots. Which tool should the engineer use?","o":["AWS Management Console","AWS CLI","AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS CloudFormation"],"a":[1],"e":"The AWS CLI runs AWS commands from a terminal and can be placed in shell scripts for scheduled automation. The Management Console is interactive and cannot be called from a cron job."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A finance manager wants to check this month's AWS charges and the state of a few EC2 instances once, from a web browser, without installing any tools. Which option should the manager use?","o":["AWS Management Console","AWS CloudFormation","AWS SDK for JavaScript","AWS CLI"],"a":[0],"e":"The AWS Management Console runs in a browser and suits quick, one-time viewing tasks. The CLI and SDKs require installation, credentials, and commands or code."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"Which statement about the AWS Management Console, the AWS CLI, and the AWS SDKs is correct?","o":["Only the AWS Management Console can create resources; the CLI and SDKs are read-only","The AWS SDKs can be used only from inside AWS Lambda functions","They all make requests to the same underlying AWS service APIs","The AWS CLI works only on Amazon EC2 instances"],"a":[2],"e":"The console, CLI, and SDKs are all front ends to the same AWS APIs, so they differ only in how you interact with AWS. The CLI and SDKs can create, change, and delete resources from any computer with network access and credentials."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A solutions architect is trying a new AWS service for the first time and wants to explore its settings through guided wizards and visual forms before deciding how to automate it. Which access method is MOST appropriate for this exploration?","o":["AWS Management Console","AWS CLI","AWS SDK for Go","AWS CloudFormation"],"a":[0],"e":"The AWS Management Console offers guided, visual workflows that suit exploring and learning a service. CloudFormation and the SDKs are better once the setup is known and needs to be automated or repeated."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"An engineering firm wants contractors to use a GPU-intensive CAD application from a web browser. The contractors must not install the application locally and do not need a full virtual desktop. Which service should the firm use?","o":["AWS Amplify","Amazon WorkSpaces","Amazon WorkSpaces Secure Browser","Amazon AppStream 2.0"],"a":[3],"e":"Amazon AppStream 2.0 (now also branded Amazon WorkSpaces Applications) streams individual desktop applications, including GPU applications, to a browser. Amazon WorkSpaces provides a full persistent desktop, which is more than the contractors need."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"A university wants students to use licensed Windows statistics software from their Chromebooks only during lab sessions. Sessions should not be persistent, and the university wants to pay only for streaming time. Which AWS service fits BEST?","o":["Amazon WorkSpaces","Amazon AppStream 2.0","Amazon Lightsail","Amazon EC2 Dedicated Hosts"],"a":[1],"e":"Amazon AppStream 2.0 streams specific applications to any device with non-persistent sessions and usage-based pricing. Amazon WorkSpaces assigns each user a persistent desktop, which is unnecessary for short lab sessions."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"A software vendor wants to offer browser-based trials of its legacy Windows desktop application without rewriting it as a web application. Which AWS service lets the vendor deliver the application this way?","o":["Amazon WorkSpaces Secure Browser","Amazon AppStream 2.0","AWS Amplify","AWS Elastic Beanstalk"],"a":[1],"e":"Amazon AppStream 2.0 runs the existing desktop application on AWS and streams it to the user's browser, so no rewrite is needed. WorkSpaces Secure Browser gives access to websites and web applications, not installed desktop applications."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"Which AWS services deliver applications or full desktops that run on AWS-managed virtual machines and stream the output to end-user devices? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon WorkSpaces","Amazon AppStream 2.0","Amazon CloudFront","AWS Amplify","AWS Outposts"],"a":[0,1],"e":"Amazon WorkSpaces streams full virtual desktops, and Amazon AppStream 2.0 streams individual applications; both run on AWS and show the output on the user's device. Amplify builds and hosts web and mobile apps that run in the user's own browser or device."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"Remote contractors need a full Windows desktop with their files and settings preserved between sessions. Company data must never be stored on the contractors' personal laptops. Which service should the company use?","o":["Amazon Lightsail","Amazon WorkSpaces Secure Browser","Amazon AppStream 2.0","Amazon WorkSpaces"],"a":[3],"e":"Amazon WorkSpaces provides persistent, managed virtual desktops where data stays in AWS. AppStream 2.0 streams individual applications and is typically non-persistent, so it does not give each user a full personal desktop."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"A company wants to replace its aging on-premises virtual desktop infrastructure (VDI) with a managed service that offers monthly or hourly pricing per desktop. Which AWS service should it use?","o":["Amazon WorkSpaces","AWS Outposts","AWS Elastic Beanstalk","Amazon AppStream 2.0"],"a":[0],"e":"Amazon WorkSpaces is a managed desktop-as-a-service that replaces VDI with monthly or hourly per-desktop billing. Outposts brings AWS infrastructure on premises; you would still have to build and run the VDI yourself."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"Developers need a persistent Linux desktop in the cloud where the tools they install remain available every time they log in from any device. Which service should they use?","o":["Amazon AppStream 2.0","Amazon WorkSpaces Secure Browser","Amazon WorkSpaces","AWS Amplify"],"a":[2],"e":"Amazon WorkSpaces offers persistent Windows and Linux desktops, so installed tools and files remain between sessions. WorkSpaces Secure Browser offers only a managed web browser session, not a desktop."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"After an acquisition, a company must quickly give 500 employees of the acquired business a corporate Windows desktop that joins the company's Microsoft Active Directory domain. Which service delivers the desktops?","o":["Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","AWS IAM Identity Center","AWS Directory Service","Amazon WorkSpaces"],"a":[3],"e":"Amazon WorkSpaces provisions managed virtual desktops that can join an Active Directory domain. AWS Directory Service can supply the Active Directory, but it does not provide the desktops themselves."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"Contractors use unmanaged personal devices and need access to internal web applications and SaaS tools. The company wants no data downloaded to those devices, no VPN, and no full virtual desktop. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Client VPN","Amazon AppStream 2.0","Amazon WorkSpaces Secure Browser","Amazon WorkSpaces"],"a":[2],"e":"Amazon WorkSpaces Secure Browser streams a managed, disposable browser session, so websites run in AWS and no data is left on the device. AWS Client VPN would give network access, but company data would still reach the personal device."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"A company's staff only use web-based applications such as a CRM and an intranet portal. The company wants the simplest, lowest-cost end-user computing option that keeps browsing isolated from the local device. Which service should it choose?","o":["Amazon WorkSpaces","Amazon WorkSpaces Secure Browser","Amazon EC2 Windows instances with Remote Desktop","Amazon AppStream 2.0"],"a":[1],"e":"When users only need websites, Amazon WorkSpaces Secure Browser is the lightest choice because it delivers just a secure browser, billed per active user. WorkSpaces delivers full desktops, which cost more and need more management than a browser-only use case requires."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"A security team wants to let employees open an internal web portal from any device while blocking clipboard copy and paste, file downloads, and printing from that session. Which AWS service provides these controls?","o":["Amazon WorkSpaces Secure Browser","Amazon Inspector","Amazon CloudFront","AWS WAF"],"a":[0],"e":"Amazon WorkSpaces Secure Browser lets administrators set policies for clipboard, file transfer, and printing in the streamed browser session. AWS WAF filters malicious HTTP requests to a web application; it cannot stop an authorized user from copying data."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"A company has two user groups. Call center staff only use a browser-based CRM from personal devices. Engineers need a full, persistent Windows desktop. Which AWS services should the company use? (Choose TWO.)","o":["AWS Amplify","AWS Client VPN","Amazon WorkSpaces Secure Browser","Amazon AppStream 2.0","Amazon WorkSpaces"],"a":[2,4],"e":"WorkSpaces Secure Browser fits users who need only web apps, and Amazon WorkSpaces gives engineers persistent full desktops. AppStream 2.0 streams individual applications in usually non-persistent sessions, so it fits neither group as well."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"A mobile team building iOS and Android apps wants to add user sign-up and sign-in, file storage, and a data API by using front-end libraries and a few lines of code, without designing the backend from scratch. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon API Gateway","Amazon Cognito","AWS Amplify","AWS Elastic Beanstalk"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS Amplify gives web and mobile developers libraries and tooling to add authentication, storage, and APIs and to deploy the backend. Amazon Cognito provides only user authentication (Amplify uses it behind the scenes), not the storage and API features."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"A front-end team connects its Git repository and wants every branch to be built and deployed automatically, each pull request to get a preview URL, and the site to be served through a global CDN. Which service provides all of this?","o":["AWS Amplify","AWS CodeBuild","AWS Elastic Beanstalk","Amazon CloudFront"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS Amplify Hosting provides Git-based CI/CD, branch and pull request previews, and global CDN hosting in one service. CloudFront is the CDN alone; it does not build or deploy code from a repository."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"A startup wants to deploy a server-side rendered (SSR) web application built with a popular JavaScript framework, with a custom domain and HTTPS, without configuring any servers. Which AWS service should it use?","o":["Amazon S3 static website hosting","Amazon Lightsail","Amazon EC2","AWS Amplify"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS Amplify Hosting supports SSR frameworks with managed hosting, custom domains, and HTTPS. S3 static website hosting serves only static files, so it cannot run server-side rendering."},
  {"d":3,"t":"devtools","q":"A front-end team wants its web app built and deployed every time developers push code, but it does not want to assemble CodePipeline, CodeBuild, Amazon S3, and Amazon CloudFront itself. Which AWS service should it use?","o":["AWS CloudFormation","AWS Amplify","AWS Step Functions","AWS X-Ray"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS Amplify packages build, deploy, and hosting for front-end and mobile apps into one managed workflow. CloudFormation could provision those separate services, but the team would still have to design and maintain the whole pipeline."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"Smart thermostats sometimes lose connectivity. The cloud application must store each device's last reported state and desired settings, and devices must sync those settings when they reconnect. Which AWS service provides this?","o":["Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS)","AWS IoT Core","Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)","Amazon Kinesis"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS IoT Core device shadows store each device's reported and desired state so applications and devices can sync even when the device is offline. SQS queues messages between software components and does not track device state."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"Factory sensors publish MQTT messages. The company wants SQL-like rules that send readings above a temperature threshold to an AWS Lambda function and store every reading in Amazon S3. Which service should receive the messages and apply these rules?","o":["Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS)","AWS Step Functions","Amazon EventBridge","AWS IoT Core"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS IoT Core accepts device MQTT messages, and its rules engine filters them with SQL statements and routes them to services such as Lambda and S3. EventBridge routes events between applications, but devices cannot connect to it over MQTT."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"Each of a company's connected devices must authenticate to AWS with its own unique X.509 certificate and be allowed to publish only to its own topics. Which service handles this device authentication and authorization?","o":["AWS IoT Core","Amazon Cognito","AWS IAM Identity Center","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS IoT Core authenticates devices with X.509 certificates and authorizes them with IoT policies at the topic level. ACM issues TLS certificates for websites and load balancers; it does not authenticate IoT devices."},
  {"d":3,"t":"iot-other","q":"A company sells connected pet feeders. It needs two-way communication so that the devices send telemetry to the cloud and the cloud sends commands, such as 'dispense food now,' back to specific devices. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)","Amazon API Gateway","AWS IoT Core","Amazon Connect"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS IoT Core provides secure two-way messaging between connected devices and the cloud. SNS sends notifications to subscribers such as email, SMS, or mobile push, but it does not manage connected devices."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"An airline wants its customer support line to understand requests such as 'I need to change my flight to Friday' and ask follow-up questions until it has the information it needs. Which AWS service provides this conversational capability?","o":["Amazon Transcribe","Amazon Polly","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Lex"],"a":[3],"e":"Amazon Lex combines speech recognition and natural-language understanding to detect intent and manage a multi-turn conversation. Amazon Transcribe only converts speech to text, without understanding intent or carrying on a dialog."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"An HR team wants a chatbot that lets employees book time off. The bot must recognize the request and collect the required details, such as start and end dates, before submitting it. Which service should the team use?","o":["Amazon Translate","Amazon Textract","Amazon Lex","Amazon Rekognition"],"a":[2],"e":"Amazon Lex builds chatbots with intents and slots, so it can prompt for each required detail. Textract extracts text and data from documents; it cannot hold a conversation."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"Which AWS service uses the same deep learning technology as Alexa to provide automatic speech recognition and natural-language understanding for building conversational interfaces?","o":["Amazon SageMaker AI","Amazon Lex","Amazon Polly","Amazon Comprehend"],"a":[1],"e":"Amazon Lex is powered by the same technology as Alexa and is used to build voice and text chatbots. Amazon Polly does the reverse job, turning text into lifelike speech."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"A pizza chain wants a text chatbot on its website that takes orders by asking for size, crust, and toppings until all required information has been collected. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Lex","Amazon Kendra","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Personalize"],"a":[0],"e":"Amazon Lex manages the conversation and fills each required slot by prompting the user. Amazon Comprehend can extract entities or sentiment from text, but it does not run a dialog with the customer."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"Developers want a generative AI assistant inside their IDE that suggests code, explains unfamiliar code, and generates unit tests. Which AWS service should they use?","o":["AWS CodeBuild","Amazon Q Business","Amazon Q Developer","Amazon SageMaker AI"],"a":[2],"e":"Amazon Q Developer is the generative AI assistant for software development, providing code suggestions, explanations, and tests in IDEs. Amazon Q Business answers business users' questions about enterprise content, not coding tasks."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"A company has dozens of applications on an old Java version. It wants an AI-powered assistant to upgrade the code to a newer Java version, including dependency and code changes. Which AWS service helps with this?","o":["Amazon Q Developer","AWS CodePipeline","AWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT)","AWS Application Migration Service"],"a":[0],"e":"Amazon Q Developer includes code transformation capabilities that upgrade Java applications to newer versions. AWS Application Migration Service rehosts servers as they are (lift and shift) and does not change application code."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"Employees want to ask natural-language questions and get answers drawn from company documents in SharePoint, Confluence, and Amazon S3. Answers must respect each employee's existing document permissions. Which service is designed for this?","o":["Amazon Q Developer","Amazon Lex","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Q Business"],"a":[3],"e":"Amazon Q Business is a generative AI assistant that connects to enterprise data sources and respects user access permissions; AWS now steers new customers to Amazon Quick for this use. Amazon Lex builds bots with intents you define, but it does not come with enterprise document connectors and permission-aware answers."},
  {"d":3,"t":"ml-ai","q":"While working in the AWS Management Console, an engineer wants to ask in plain language why an EC2 instance cannot connect to an RDS database and get troubleshooting guidance about the AWS resources. Which AWS service provides this help?","o":["Amazon Lex","Amazon Q Developer","AWS Health Dashboard","AWS Trusted Advisor"],"a":[1],"e":"Amazon Q Developer is available in the AWS Management Console to answer questions about AWS and help troubleshoot resources, such as network connectivity issues. Trusted Advisor runs predefined best-practice checks and does not answer free-form questions."},
  {"d":3,"t":"analytics","q":"Executives want interactive business intelligence dashboards built on data in Amazon Redshift and Amazon Athena. The solution must scale to thousands of users without managing servers and use per-user pricing. Which service should the company use?","o":["AWS Glue","Amazon Quick","Amazon CloudWatch dashboards","Amazon Redshift"],"a":[1],"e":"Amazon Quick (formerly Amazon QuickSight) delivers serverless BI dashboards with per-user pricing. Amazon Redshift stores and queries the data, but it is not a dashboard tool for business users."},
  {"d":3,"t":"analytics","q":"Business analysts want their sales dashboards to show ML-powered anomaly detection and forecasts without building or training machine learning models. Which service provides this?","o":["Amazon SageMaker AI","Amazon Kinesis","AWS Glue","Amazon Quick"],"a":[3],"e":"Amazon Quick includes built-in ML insights, such as anomaly detection and forecasting, in BI dashboards. SageMaker AI is for building, training, and deploying custom ML models, which requires data science work."},
  {"d":3,"t":"analytics","q":"A company wants to query data in Amazon S3 with standard SQL without loading it into a database and then show the results in interactive dashboards for managers. Which AWS services should it use? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon Neptune","Amazon Athena","Amazon Quick","AWS IoT Core","AWS Batch"],"a":[1,2],"e":"Amazon Athena runs serverless SQL directly on S3 data, and Amazon Quick visualizes the results in dashboards. AWS Batch runs compute jobs; it neither queries data with SQL nor builds visuals."},
  {"d":3,"t":"analytics","q":"A company wants one AI-powered workspace where employees can use BI dashboards, run research agents across internal and web data, and automate routine business workflows. Which AWS service is designed for this?","o":["Amazon Quick","Amazon SageMaker AI","Amazon Q Developer","AWS Step Functions"],"a":[0],"e":"Amazon Quick combines BI (Quick Sight) with AI agents for research and workflow automation for business users. Amazon Q Developer is aimed at software developers and AWS builders, not business analytics."},
  {"d":3,"t":"analytics","q":"A finance department needs formatted, pixel-perfect paginated reports generated from its business data and emailed to stakeholders on a schedule. Which AWS analytics service supports this?","o":["Amazon Athena","Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES)","Amazon Quick","AWS Cost Explorer"],"a":[2],"e":"Amazon Quick supports paginated reports and scheduled email delivery of reports and dashboards. Amazon SES can send email, but it cannot build reports from business data."},
  {"d":3,"t":"analytics","q":"A company runs a self-managed Elasticsearch cluster for log search and wants to move to a managed AWS service that keeps the same search and dashboard capabilities while AWS handles patching and scaling. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Glue","Amazon Athena","Amazon Redshift","Amazon OpenSearch Service"],"a":[3],"e":"Amazon OpenSearch Service is the managed service for OpenSearch and legacy Elasticsearch, with OpenSearch Dashboards for visualization. Athena runs SQL queries on S3, but it does not provide a search index or relevance-ranked full-text search."},
  {"d":3,"t":"analytics","q":"A team is building a generative AI application that must store vector embeddings and run k-nearest neighbor (k-NN) similarity searches to find semantically related documents. Which AWS analytics service supports this?","o":["Amazon Quick","Amazon Athena","Amazon OpenSearch Service","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams"],"a":[2],"e":"Amazon OpenSearch Service supports vector search with k-NN, so it is often used as a vector store for semantic search and retrieval-augmented generation. Kinesis Data Streams ingests streaming data, but it cannot index or search it."},
  {"d":3,"t":"analytics","q":"A company wants to ingest clickstream logs continuously and make them searchable and visible in dashboards within seconds, with minimal infrastructure management. Which AWS services should it use? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon Data Firehose","Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES)","Amazon Neptune","Amazon OpenSearch Service","AWS Batch"],"a":[0,3],"e":"Amazon Data Firehose delivers streaming data directly to Amazon OpenSearch Service, which indexes it for near real-time search and dashboards. AWS Batch runs scheduled compute jobs and does not suit continuous, seconds-level ingestion."},
  {"d":3,"t":"analytics","q":"A legal team needs to search millions of contract documents for keywords and phrases and get results ranked by relevance, with highlighting of matching text. Which service is MOST appropriate?","o":["Amazon Textract","Amazon OpenSearch Service","Amazon Redshift","Amazon Athena"],"a":[1],"e":"Amazon OpenSearch Service provides full-text indexing, relevance ranking, and highlighting. Amazon Textract can extract text from scanned contracts, but it does not index or search that text."},
  {"d":3,"t":"integration","q":"A company wants to orchestrate an ML pipeline that runs an AWS Glue ETL job, then starts an Amazon SageMaker AI training job, and then sends an Amazon SNS notification. The steps must run in order and call AWS services directly with little custom code. Which service should coordinate the pipeline?","o":["AWS Step Functions","AWS CodePipeline","Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS)","Amazon EventBridge"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS Step Functions orchestrates multi-step workflows with direct integrations to many AWS services and tracks the state of each step. EventBridge routes individual events to targets, but it does not manage a multi-step sequence and its state."},
  {"d":3,"t":"integration","q":"Developers chain several AWS Lambda functions together by having each function call the next one directly. Error handling and retries are duplicated in every function and are hard to maintain. Which service should replace this coordination logic?","o":["Amazon API Gateway","Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)","AWS Step Functions","AWS Lambda with a longer timeout"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS Step Functions moves sequencing, retries, and error handling out of the function code into a managed state machine. SNS fans out messages to subscribers but does not track a multi-step process or retry its steps."},
  {"d":3,"t":"integration","q":"A company must process 10,000 files in Amazon S3 by running the same AWS Lambda logic on each file in parallel and then combine the results. It wants a low-code, visual workflow that manages the parallelism. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Step Functions","Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS)","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS Step Functions has a Map state (including distributed mode) that runs steps in parallel for many items and collects the results in a visual workflow. SQS can buffer work items, but it does not orchestrate parallel steps or combine their output."},
  {"d":3,"t":"integration","q":"A company needs to orchestrate high-volume, short-duration workflows that process streaming IoT events, each completing in a few seconds, at the lowest cost. Which option should it use?","o":["Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) FIFO queues","Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)","AWS Step Functions Standard Workflows","AWS Step Functions Express Workflows"],"a":[3],"e":"Step Functions Express Workflows are designed for high-volume, short-duration event processing and are priced for that scale. Standard Workflows suit long-running workflows (up to a year) with exactly-once execution and cost more at high volume."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A company exposes Lambda-based microservices to partners. It needs request throttling, response caching, and request validation at a single managed front door. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Global Accelerator","Amazon API Gateway","Amazon Route 53","AWS Transit Gateway"],"a":[1],"e":"Amazon API Gateway is the managed front door for APIs, with throttling, caching, and request validation. Route 53 only resolves domain names to endpoints; it does not inspect or throttle API requests."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A serverless API must accept only requests from users who have signed in through an Amazon Cognito user pool. What is the simplest way to enforce this?","o":["Put the API in an Amazon Route 53 private hosted zone","Use Amazon API Gateway with an Amazon Cognito user pool authorizer","Create an AWS WAF rule that checks usernames","Add a security group rule to the AWS Lambda functions"],"a":[1],"e":"Amazon API Gateway can validate Cognito user pool tokens with a built-in authorizer before a request reaches the backend. AWS WAF filters requests by patterns such as IP address or SQL injection, but it does not validate a user's sign-in tokens."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A company wants to present a legacy on-premises HTTP service and several new Lambda microservices as one unified API, with separate dev and prod stages and versioning. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Transit Gateway","AWS PrivateLink","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon API Gateway"],"a":[3],"e":"Amazon API Gateway can route API paths to different backends (Lambda or HTTP endpoints) and manage stages and deployments. Transit Gateway connects networks and VPCs at the network layer; it does not create or manage APIs."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A web application must accept orders through an HTTPS API and buffer them so that backend workers can process them later, even during traffic spikes. Which AWS services should the company combine? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon API Gateway","Amazon Connect","Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS)","AWS Batch","Amazon Neptune"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Amazon API Gateway receives the HTTPS requests and can send them directly to an Amazon SQS queue, which buffers orders for workers to process. AWS Batch runs compute jobs, but it does not accept API requests or act as a durable message buffer."},
];
